(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["fetal"]="<h1>Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-fetus-need-shortcuts\">1. Why does the fetus need shortcuts?</h2>\n<p>Before birth, the <strong><span data-term=\"placenta\">placenta</span></strong>, not the lungs, does gas exchange. The fetal lungs are filled with fluid, and their vessels are tightly constricted, so <strong>pulmonary resistance is very high</strong>. Pumping all the blood through them would be wasted effort.</p>\n<p>So the fetal circulation has <strong>three shortcuts (shunts)</strong> that send oxygen-rich blood where it is needed, mainly the heart and brain, and route most of it <strong>past the liver and lungs</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Anatomical illustration tracing placenta, umbilical veins and arteries, fetal heart, ductus venosus, foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Complete fetal circulation.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2916_Fetal_Circulatory_System-02.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-trace-the-fetal-circuit\">2. Trace the fetal circuit</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Placenta → umbilical vein:</strong> the most oxygen-rich blood in the fetus (yet still only about 80% saturated) travels up the <strong>single umbilical vein</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Shortcut 1: <span data-term=\"ductus-venosus\">ductus venosus</span>.</strong> Much of it <strong>bypasses the liver</strong>, going straight to the inferior vena cava.</li>\n<li><strong>Into the right atrium.</strong> The stream from the IVC is aimed straight across the atrium at an opening in the septum.</li>\n<li><strong>Shortcut 2: <span data-term=\"foramen-ovale\">foramen ovale</span>.</strong> This oxygen-rich stream crosses <strong>from the right atrium to the left atrium</strong>, then goes to the left ventricle → aorta → <strong>heart and brain first</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SVC blood</strong> (oxygen-poor, from the head) goes mainly into the right ventricle → pulmonary trunk.</li>\n<li><strong>Shortcut 3: <span data-term=\"ductus-arteriosus\">ductus arteriosus</span>.</strong> Pulmonary resistance is high, so most of this blood <strong>bypasses the lungs</strong>, crossing <strong>from the pulmonary trunk into the descending aorta</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Back to the placenta:</strong> the <strong>two umbilical arteries</strong> (branches of the internal iliac arteries) carry blood back to be reoxygenated.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure. Three fetal vascular shortcuts</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 171\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Ductus venosus bypasses liver sinusoids, foramen ovale connects right atrium with left atrium, and ductus arteriosus connects pulmonary trunk with descending aorta\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#24354d\"><rect x=\"7\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"121\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#ecf3ff\" stroke=\"#6f97c8\"/><rect x=\"229\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"121\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf5ee\" stroke=\"#76a988\"/><rect x=\"451\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"121\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff1dc\" stroke=\"#bb914f\"/><g font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\"><text x=\"107\" y=\"53\">Ductus venosus</text><text x=\"329\" y=\"53\">Foramen ovale</text><text x=\"551\" y=\"53\">Ductus arteriosus</text></g><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"107\" y=\"84\">Umbilical vein → IVC</text><text x=\"107\" y=\"107\">Partial liver bypass</text><text x=\"329\" y=\"84\">Right atrium → left</text><text x=\"329\" y=\"107\">atrium</text><text x=\"551\" y=\"84\">Pulmonary trunk →</text><text x=\"551\" y=\"107\">descending aorta</text></g></g></svg><p>Original route schematic; arrows show preferential fetal flow, not every stream or complete anatomical connection. Increased pulmonary flow after birth changes the pressure gradients that maintain these pathways.</p></figure>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Shunt</th>\n  <th>Connects</th>\n  <th>Bypasses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n  <td>Umbilical vein → inferior vena cava</td>\n  <td>The liver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></td>\n  <td>Right atrium → left atrium</td>\n  <td>The lungs (via the right ventricle)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n  <td>Pulmonary trunk → aorta</td>\n  <td>The lungs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does blood flow right-to-left through the shunts?</strong> Because, before birth, the <strong>right side</strong> has the higher pressure: pulmonary resistance is high, while the placenta keeps systemic resistance low.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-fetal-haemoglobin-grabbing-oxygen-from-the-mother\">3. Fetal haemoglobin: grabbing oxygen from the mother</h2>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"fetal-haemoglobin\">Fetal haemoglobin (HbF)</span></strong> holds oxygen <strong>more tightly</strong> than adult haemoglobin (HbA), because it binds the molecule <strong><span data-term=\"bpg\">2,3-BPG</span></strong> less strongly. 2,3-BPG normally loosens haemoglobin's grip on oxygen. So, at the low oxygen levels in the placenta, fetal blood can <strong>pull oxygen across from the mother's blood</strong>. After birth, HbF is gradually replaced by HbA over several months.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why is most blood diverted away from the fetal lungs?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The fetal lungs are fluid-filled and have very high vascular resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The fetal lungs already receive oxygen from amniotic fluid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The fetal heart is too weak to pump blood through the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The lungs would take oxygen away from the placenta</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Gas exchange happens in the placenta. The fluid-filled lungs' constricted vessels resist flow, so blood takes the shunts.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Which shunt carries blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ductus venosus</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The foramen ovale</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ductus arteriosus</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The umbilical artery</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The ductus arteriosus links the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta, bypassing the lungs.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why does fetal haemoglobin help the fetus take oxygen from the mother?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It binds 2,3-BPG more strongly, so it releases oxygen easily</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It holds oxygen more tightly because it binds 2,3-BPG less strongly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It carries carbon dioxide instead of oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It is found only in the placenta, not in fetal blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> With less 2,3-BPG bound, HbF has a higher affinity for oxygen, so it loads oxygen from maternal blood.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-what-closes-after-delivery\">4. What closes after delivery?</h2>\n<p>Two events at birth flip the pressures:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The first breaths:</strong> the lungs fill with air and oxygen rises, so the lung vessels <strong>dilate</strong> and <strong>pulmonary resistance falls sharply</strong>. Lung blood flow surges, and much more blood returns to the <strong>left atrium</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Clamping the cord:</strong> the placenta, a <strong>low-resistance</strong> circuit, is cut off, so <strong>systemic resistance rises</strong>. The flow up the umbilical vein stops.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>At birth — two changes that redirect circulation</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 220\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"First breaths decrease pulmonary vascular resistance and increase left atrial return, while cord clamping removes low resistance placenta and raises systemic resistance, leading to functional shunt closure\"><g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#253e5b\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"12\" width=\"315\" height=\"78\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e4f2ff\" stroke=\"#76a1c8\"/><rect x=\"337\" y=\"12\" width=\"315\" height=\"78\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#eaf5e9\" stroke=\"#75a884\"/><g font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\"><text x=\"165\" y=\"40\">First breaths</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"40\">Cord clamping</text></g><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"165\" y=\"65\">Pulmonary resistance falls</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"65\">Placental circuit removed</text></g><rect x=\"8\" y=\"130\" width=\"315\" height=\"73\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#f0f6ff\" stroke=\"#76a1c8\"/><rect x=\"337\" y=\"130\" width=\"315\" height=\"73\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#f1f8ed\" stroke=\"#75a884\"/><g font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"165\" y=\"156\">Lung flow and left atrial</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"174\">return increase</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"156\">Systemic resistance rises</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"174\">Umbilical venous flow stops</text></g><g stroke=\"#53778c\" stroke-width=\"3\"><path d=\"M165 90v39M158 120l7 9 7 -9M495 90v39M488 120l7 9 7 -9\"/></g></g></svg><p>These simultaneous changes shift atrial pressures, favor functional foramen ovale closure and contribute to ductus arteriosus constriction. Anatomical closure occurs later and varies by structure.</p></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Medical illustration of neonatal heart circulation showing postnatal pulmonary blood flow and altered fetal shunt pathways\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Circulation after birth.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neonatal_Heart_Circulation.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Now <strong>the left side has the higher pressure</strong>, and each shunt closes:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Shunt</th>\n  <th>Why it closes</th>\n  <th>Becomes (anatomical remnant)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></td>\n  <td>Left atrial pressure now <strong>exceeds</strong> right, which presses the flap shut</td>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"fossa-ovalis\">Fossa ovalis</span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Higher oxygen</strong> and <strong>less prostaglandin</strong> (from the placenta) make it constrict</td>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"ligamentum-arteriosum\">Ligamentum arteriosum</span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n  <td>No more umbilical venous flow</td>\n  <td><strong>Ligamentum venosum</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Umbilical vein</td>\n  <td>No more flow</td>\n  <td><strong>Ligamentum teres</strong> (of the liver)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Umbilical arteries</td>\n  <td>No more flow</td>\n  <td><strong>Medial umbilical ligaments</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Closure happens in two steps:</strong> <strong>functional</strong> closure within minutes to days, as flow stops; then <strong>anatomical</strong> closure over weeks to months, as tissue seals the channel.</p>\n<p><strong>Understanding Check: Section 4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does the foramen ovale close at birth?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Prostaglandin levels rise after the cord is clamped</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Left atrial pressure becomes higher than right atrial pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The umbilical vein carries more blood after birth</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pulmonary resistance rises when the lungs fill with air</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> More blood returns from the lungs to the left atrium, and its pressure rises above the right, pressing the flap shut.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What makes the ductus arteriosus constrict after birth?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Rising oxygen and falling prostaglandins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Falling oxygen and rising prostaglandins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A rise in pulmonary vascular resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Increased flow through the umbilical arteries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Oxygen constricts the ductus, and the prostaglandins that kept it open fall once the placenta is gone.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> At birth, pulmonary resistance falls while systemic resistance rises. Why do both happen?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both are caused by the first breath alone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Breathing dilates the lung vessels; cord clamping removes the low-resistance placenta</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Both are caused by clamping the cord alone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The ductus arteriosus closing raises both resistances</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> They are two separate events: lung aeration lowers pulmonary resistance, and losing the placenta raises systemic resistance.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-chapter-essentials\">5. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The placenta</strong> does fetal gas exchange. The fluid-filled lungs have high resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>Three shunts:</strong> ductus venosus (bypasses the liver), foramen ovale (right → left atrium), ductus arteriosus (pulmonary trunk → aorta).</li>\n<li><strong>The best-oxygenated blood</strong> goes through the foramen ovale to the heart and brain.</li>\n<li><strong>Before birth the right side has the higher pressure</strong>, so flow through the shunts is right-to-left.</li>\n<li><strong>HbF</strong> binds oxygen more tightly (less 2,3-BPG binding), so it loads oxygen from the mother.</li>\n<li><strong>At birth:</strong> the first breaths lower pulmonary resistance; cord clamping raises systemic resistance; <strong>left pressure &gt; right</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Remnants:</strong> fossa ovalis, ligamentum arteriosum, ligamentum venosum, ligamentum teres, medial umbilical ligaments.</li>\n</ol>\n";
